      "guide": "references/facts-device.md",
      "source": "kernels/ascriptor_kernels/attention/a5_v8_cube_stage",
      "api_guide": "library/docs/api/cube.md",
      "note": "Two readings, in this order. The demonstration is here: `kernels/stage.py` drains one L0C tile twice — SPLITN into a per-sub-block `ub_score`, SINGLE into the whole `ub_full` that only sub-block 0 stores — and publishes both to GM, so `score2` is the two 64-query halves concatenated and `score3` is the complete tile. Then the production use, in `attention.a5_pfa_hif8_metadata`: grep `DualMode.SPLITM` in `kernels/metadata.py` (line 443 today). That README does not name it, which is how reading the unit for its task table can leave the drain unnoticed.",
      "device": "a5"
    },
    {
      "id": "mask-write-semantics",
      "terms": [
        "zeroing",
        "merging",
        "mask write",
        "masked lane",
        "inactive lane",
        "expsub",
        "零化",
        "掩码写",
        "掩码写语义",
        "非活动 lane"
      ],
      "guide": "references/precision.md",
      "source": "library/docs/api/mask-write-semantics.md",
      "case": null,
      "device": "a5",
      "note": "Six roles over the 66 operators that take a `mask`. The two a kernel most easily gets backwards are opposite: masked arithmetic writes 0 into the lane it turns off, and a masked store does not write that lane at all, so the memory keeps its bytes. Nothing preserves an inactive destination register lane. A worked example of the zeroing half is `kernels/attention/a5_presence_mask`, where dropping the mask off the `expsub` is caught by every declared case."
    },
    {
      "id": "device-facts",
      "terms": [
        "core count",
        "cube cores",
        "vector cores",
        "AIC",
        "block_dim",
        "UB size",
        "L0C size",
        "核数",
